WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:06.080
Speaker 1: Let's go next to&nbsp;
S.D. from Dallas, Texas. S.D.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.080 --> 00:00:10.080
you're on the air.
Speaker 3: I even.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:22.320
What is your take on so the states are now making&nbsp;
the new maps for all the legislative districts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.320 --> 00:00:29.829
and I want to know what's your take so far&nbsp;
on the gerrymandering for this coming decade?&nbsp;

00:00:29.829 --> 00:00:34.720
Speaker 1: I've not read that much about it other&nbsp;
than there are some interesting districts that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.280
could just be eliminated, like, for example,&nbsp;
Lauren Bowerbirds district. I guess if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:45.920
proposed map is accepted, she may just&nbsp;
not have a district anymore. I've not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:52.160
you know, one of the things I'm trying to do is&nbsp;
when there is like maybes, let's wait and see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:56.400
if something is happening, unless there's really&nbsp;
something specific that could be done to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:00.800
the course. And with a lot of the gerrymandering&nbsp;
and district drawing that's being proposed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.440 --> 00:01:04.480
we're still waiting to see like what are the&nbsp;
final submissions and are they even really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.480 --> 00:01:08.880
going to be accepted? So I don't know. I mean,&nbsp;
what's your take? Is it are there particularly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:14.000
egregious examples that we should be looking at?
Speaker 3: Oh, yes. Oh, yes. Yeah. You can look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:23.280
at states like like Texas and Ohio. It is like&nbsp;
absolutely egregious. The the state legislature&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.280 --> 00:01:32.480
are absolutely certain, the mid-February. But I&nbsp;
guess I was really more looking into is knowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:36.880
that the gerrymandering is going to be pretty&nbsp;
egregious across a lot of these red states.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.160
Is it worth dropping the principle of&nbsp;
no gerrymandering in blue states and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:46.160
just fighting fire with fire? At this point?
Speaker 1: I know this is always the thing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.120
which is if the Republicans are just going&nbsp;
to keep doing it in the states they control,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:56.960
isn't it unilateral disarmament to say, all right,&nbsp;
well, we'll draw them fairly in the blue states&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.680 --> 00:02:04.800
and then they'll draw them completely unfairly&nbsp;
in the red states? I think on this, for as long&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.800 --> 00:02:11.680
as these are the rules, Democrats have no choice&nbsp;
but to use the system to their advantage when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:18.480
can, because otherwise we will never even get the&nbsp;
opportunity to change how this is done. But the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.560
goal has to be to change the system. And then, of&nbsp;
course, you get to this question of our people who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:27.520
got in because of the system. We have really&nbsp;
going to be motivated to change it. And that's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:30.960
a very legitimate question, which we addressed,&nbsp;
I think it was last week on the show. But no,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:36.960
I mean, listen, if if Democrats just say let's&nbsp;
drop perfectly square districts everywhere and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:41.760
do everything perfectly and then Republicans keep&nbsp;
doing like they're Dan Crenshaw district, it's one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:47.760
of the most egregious examples of Republicans do,&nbsp;
then it just further makes it so that Democrats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.760 --> 00:02:51.600
will never actually be in power and able to&nbsp;
really deal with this issue. So it's unfortunate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:54.400
but it is what it is.
Speaker 3:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.400 --> 00:02:58.240
Absolutely right on, David, thank you very much.
Speaker 1: My pleasure. Thank you so much for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:02.560
the call. Very important question. Very&nbsp;
important question. There are areas where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:08.080
we say we're not going to stoop to that&nbsp;
level because then we are as bad as them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

